    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">
      Galvanized vs PVC-Coated Wire Mesh: Cost, Lifespan, and When to Choose Each
    </h1>

    <p class="text-theme-secondary text-lg mb-8">
      Last updated: September 2026 | 9 min read
    </p>

    <!-- Answer First -->
    <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-8">
      <p class="text-theme-primary text-lg leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Quick answer:</strong> Choose <strong>hot-dip galvanized wire mesh</strong> when you need the best corrosion protection per dollar for outdoor structural use—fences, gabions, machine guards, construction reinforcement. It costs roughly $0.30–0.80/m² and lasts 15–25 years in rural air. Choose <strong>PVC-coated wire mesh</strong> when the mesh is visible, handled by people, or exposed to chemicals, salt spray, or constant moisture—the plastic coating roughly doubles the lifespan of the underlying galvanized wire and costs about 40–70% more. Avoid plain electro-galvanized mesh for anything outdoors; its 10–40 g/m² zinc layer can show red rust within 2–5 years. Use the <a href="/tools/welded-wire-mesh-weight-calculator/" class="text-theme-primary underline">weight calculator</a> to verify the coating hasn't been skimped, and the <a href="/tools/fob-price-calculator/" class="text-theme-primary underline">FOB price calculator</a> to compare quotes on equal terms.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- TOC -->
    <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Table of Contents</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-theme-secondary">
