    <!-- Intro -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        "Galvanized" and "PVC-coated" are not single products—they are families of finishes with wildly different performance. A roll of electro-galvanized welded mesh and a roll of hot-dip galvanized welded mesh can look identical on day one, yet one rusts through in three years while the other lasts twenty. This guide breaks down the three coating systems you will actually encounter from <a href="/wire-mesh-hebei/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">Hebei factories</a>, what each costs per square meter, how long each lasts in real environments (not lab salt-spray hours), and how to write the specification so the factory cannot quietly downgrade your coating.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Systems -->
    <section id="systems" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">The Three Coating Systems Explained</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Nearly every "galvanized vs PVC" comparison skips the most important fact: there are two completely different galvanizing processes, and they sit at opposite ends of the quality scale.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-3 mt-6">Electro-Galvanized (EG / Pre-Galvanized)</h3>
      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Wire is drawn, then coated with zinc by electroplating in a cold bath. The result is a smooth, bright, shiny wire—but the zinc layer is only <strong>10–40 g/m²</strong> (roughly 1.5–6 µm thick). Electro-galvanized wire mesh is cheap, pretty, and fine for indoor use: shelving, machine guards, rodent screens, ceiling grids. Outside, the thin zinc sacrifices itself within a few years. Welded mesh made from pre-galvanized wire also suffers from "burn-off" at the weld points—the welding heat vaporizes the zinc exactly where corrosion protection matters most.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-3">Hot-Dip Galvanized (HDG / Post-Galvanized)</h3>
      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Finished mesh is dipped in molten zinc at about 450 °C, creating a metallurgical bond and a coating of <strong>200–400 g/m²</strong> (roughly 30–55 µm). For welded mesh, the weld points get coated too, which fixes the burn-off problem. Hot-dip mesh has the familiar spangled, slightly rough look. Relevant standards: ASTM A641 for zinc-coated wire, ASTM A974 for welded wire mesh fabric, and ASTM A123/A153 for hardware and assemblies. This is the workhorse coating for <a href="/applications/fence/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">fencing</a>, gabions, and construction mesh.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-3">PVC-Coated (Vinyl-Coated)</h3>
      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Galvanized wire—usually hot-dip, sometimes electro—is extrusion-coated with a layer of PVC (polyvinyl chloride), typically 0.4–1.0 mm thick, applied as powder (dip) or liquid (spray). The plastic adds four things: a moisture barrier over the zinc, UV-stable color (green RAL 6005 and black are the stock colors), a softer surface that won't scratch handlers, and electrical insulation. The tradeoffs: higher price, lower maximum service temperature (PVC starts degrading above 60–70 °C), and brittleness in extreme cold if the factory used cheap unplasticized PVC. Quality factories add UV stabilizers and plasticizers; ask for the coating thickness in writing—"PVC coated" with a 0.3 mm skin is a different product than a full 1.0 mm coat.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Galfan: The Fourth Option Worth Knowing</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          Galfan (Zn-5%Al mischmetal) coatings give 2–3× the corrosion resistance of pure-zinc hot-dip at only a small price premium. If your supplier offers "zinc-aluminum coated" wire under ASTM A641 Class options or EN 10244-2, take the quote seriously for coastal or high-moisture jobs.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

