      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Two cost traps to note. First, <strong>coating weight is the cheapest thing to fake</strong>: dropping from 275 g/m² to 180 g/m² of zinc saves the factory perhaps $0.05/m² while cutting outdoor life nearly in half. Second, PVC-coated mesh is quoted by <em>finished</em> wire diameter—"2.5 mm PVC-coated" usually means 1.8–2.0 mm steel core plus 0.5 mm of plastic. If you need a specific steel core diameter for strength, specify the core and the coating separately in your RFQ.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Lifespan -->
    <section id="lifespan" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Lifespan in Real Environments</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Lab salt-spray hours (ASTM B117) are how factories advertise, but they map poorly to field life. The table below shows realistic time-to-first-rust for a mesh installed outdoors, based on typical corrosion categories from ISO 9223 / EN 10147 logic and field reports from contractors:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Environment (ISO 9223 category)</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Electro-Galvanized (25 g/m²)</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Hot-Dip (275 g/m²)</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">PVC-Coated on HDG</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Indoor / dry (C1)</td>
              <td class="p-3">10+ years</td>
              <td class="p-3">50+ years</td>
              <td class="p-3">50+ years</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Rural, low pollution (C2)</td>
              <td class="p-3">2–5 years</td>
              <td class="p-3">20–30 years</td>
              <td class="p-3">30–50 years</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Urban / industrial (C3–C4)</td>
              <td class="p-3">1–3 years</td>
              <td class="p-3">10–20 years</td>
              <td class="p-3">20–35 years</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Coastal / marine (C5)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Under 1 year</td>
              <td class="p-3">5–10 years</td>
              <td class="p-3">10–20 years</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        PVC wins in the field for a simple reason: even when the plastic gets nicked at a weld or handling point, the remaining skin slows moisture reaching the zinc, and the zinc slows it reaching the steel. It is a redundant system. That is why <a href="/products/chain-link-fence/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">chain-link fencing</a> for parks, schools, and sports grounds is almost always PVC-coated, while the same fence on a remote farm boundary is usually plain hot-dip.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Temperature and Chemical Limits</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          PVC softens above ~60–70 °C and can become brittle below −20 °C if cheaply formulated—relevant for machine guards near ovens, engine compartments, or cold-storage installs. Hot-dip zinc handles 200 °C fine but is attacked by strong alkalis and acid runoff. For chemical plants and food processing with washdown chemicals, consider <a href="/products/stainless-steel-wire-mesh/" class="text-theme-primary underline">stainless steel mesh</a> instead—our <a href="/blog/304-vs-316-stainless-steel-mesh/" class="text-theme-primary underline">304 vs 316 guide</a> covers the choice.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Decision -->
