              <td class="p-3">Cheapest; no weather exposure</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Farm fencing, field boundary, gabion baskets</td>
              <td class="p-3">Hot-dip ≥275 g/m²</td>
              <td class="p-3">Best $/year outdoors; weld points protected</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Sports grounds, schools, residential fencing</td>
              <td class="p-3">PVC-coated on HDG wire</td>
              <td class="p-3">Looks finished, safe to touch, long life</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Coastal or chemical plant perimeter</td>
              <td class="p-3">PVC-coated, or Galfan HDG</td>
              <td class="p-3">Redundant barrier against salt/chlorides</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Concrete reinforcement (SMA)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Plain or lightly galvanized per code</td>
              <td class="p-3">See our <a href="/applications/concrete/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">concrete application guide</a> for code rules</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">High-temperature or abrasive service</td>
              <td class="p-3">Hot-dip galvanized (not PVC)</td>
              <td class="p-3">PVC melts/abrasion-shreds; zinc tolerates heat</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Budget rule of thumb: if the installation labor or replacement access costs more than the mesh itself—true for almost any fence—buy the better coating. A $0.25/m² saving that halves service life is the most expensive decision in this industry.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Verify -->
    <section id="verify" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">How to Verify You Got the Coating You Paid For</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Coating downgrade is the single most common quality failure in galvanized mesh imports. Protect yourself with three cheap tests:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Weigh the mesh.</strong> Zinc adds ~7 g/m² per µm of thickness on both wire surfaces. Take a 1 m² sample, strip nothing—just compare its actual weight to the theoretical weight of bare wire from the <a href="/tools/welded-wire-mesh-weight-calculator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">welded mesh weight calculator</a>. A systematic shortfall of more than ~3–5% after accounting for tolerance means thin wire, light zinc, or both.</li>
        <li><strong>Measure coating thickness.</strong> A magnetic eddy-current coating gauge costs $40–80 and reads zinc or PVC thickness non-destructively. Take 10+ readings per roll, including at weld points.</li>
        <li><strong>Strip test on samples.</strong> Dissolve the zinc from a known wire length in dilute hydrochloric acid (the zinc dissolves; the steel doesn't) and weigh before/after to get true g/m². Any university materials lab or SGS office will do this for ~$30–50.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Write the verification method into the contract: "Zinc coating weight per single-side strip test, minimum 275 g/m², ASTM A90/A90M method." Then a pre-shipment inspector can enforce it, and a dispute has a defined technical referee.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Is PVC-coated wire mesh stronger than galvanized?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Not in load terms—the steel core carries the strength, and "2.5 mm PVC-coated" wire usually has only a 1.8–2.0 mm steel core. But PVC is more <em>durable</em> in service: it resists scratches, moisture, and handling damage better, so the underlying steel corrodes slower. If strength matters, specify the bare steel core diameter, e.g. "2.0 mm core + 0.5 mm PVC = 3.0 mm finished."
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">How long does hot-dip galvanized wire mesh really last outdoors?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            With a proper commercial-class coating (≥275 g/m²), expect 20–30 years in rural air, 10–20 years in urban/industrial air, and 5–10 years within a few kilometers of the sea. Zinc corrodes sacrificially, so the clock starts only after the coating is consumed—thicker zinc is literally a longer battery.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Can I paint over galvanized or PVC-coated mesh?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Yes on hot-dip galvanized mesh: degrease, lightly sweep-blast or use a etch primer (zinc phosphate or "galvanized metal primer"), then topcoat. Paint on PVC is unreliable unless the PVC is factory-primed—if you want a custom color, ask the factory for powder-coated or liquid-painted finish directly; most Anping lines offer any RAL color at a small surcharge.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What does "zinc coating 40 g/m²" mean in factory quotes—and is it enough?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            It means 40 grams of zinc per square meter of wire surface, roughly 5–6 µm thick—typical of electro-galvanized or "light hot-dip" wire. Indoors, fine. Outdoors, expect first rust in 2–5 years. For exterior structural use, insist on ≥200 g/m² (hot-dip commercial class) and put the number plus the test method (ASTM A90) in the contract.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Does PVC coating fade or crack in the sun?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Cheap PVC without UV stabilizers fades and chalks within 3–5 years and can crack in extreme cold. Quality export-grade PVC is UV-stabilized and holds color 8–12 years. Ask the factory for the PVC compound spec and UV hours; reputable Hebei suppliers quote 1,000+ hour QUV testing for export products. Dark green and black hide fading best.
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

